{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DKTar2fQbVzty1pLX3hGBq","type":"tool_result","content":"usage: answer.py [-h] [--repo REPO] [--met {yes,no,unknown}] [--evidence EVIDENCE] [--quote QUOTE] [--counter COUNTER] [--note NOTE]\n                 [--rating {likely_pass,gap,needs_evidence,na_candidate}] [--confidence {high,medium,low}] [--summary SUMMARY] [--next NEXT_STEP] [--violates VIOLATES]\n                 [--also-affects ALSO_AFFECTS] [--verdict {confirmed,dismissed}] [--item ITEM] [--reason REASON]\n                 findings {check,flag,item,prescan,next} [target]\n\nWrite one answer into a findings file, check it, and show what's next.\n\nUse this instead of editing the JSON by hand.\n\n  Answer a check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json check B05.2 --met no --evidence src/x.py:41 \\\n        --quote \"exact code on that line\" --note \"what the code does\" [--counter \"what you searched\"]\n\n  Add a red flag:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json flag B05 --evidence src/x.py:41 --note \"what's wrong\"\n\n  Finish an item:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json item B05 --rating gap --confidence high \\\n        --summary \"one sentence\" --next \"what to fix\" [--violates \"exact words from the requirement\"] \\\n        [--also-affects E06]\n\n  Resolve a pre-scan hit:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json prescan H003 --verdict confirmed --item R03 --reason \"...\"\n\n  Show the next unanswered check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json next\n\nThe quote is checked against the file right away; a \"yes\" without a matching quote\nand a counter-search is refused. Paths are relative to the repo, which defaults to the\nfolder holding FINDINGS.json (set --repo to change it).\n\npositional arguments:\n  findings\n  {check,flag,item,prescan,next}\n  target\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --repo REPO\n  --met {yes,no,unknown}\n  --evidence EVIDENCE\n  --quote QUOTE\n  --counter COUNTER\n  --note NOTE\n  --rating {likely_pass,gap,needs_evidence,na_candidate}\n  --confidence {high,medium,low}\n  --summary SUMMARY\n  --next NEXT_STEP\n  --violates VIOLATES\n  --also-affects ALSO_AFFECTS\n  --verdict {confirmed,dismissed}\n  --item ITEM\n  --reason REASON\nusage: review_coverage.py [-h] {inventory,policy,ledger} ...\n\nStatic candidate inventory and evidence ledger. Never executes repository code.\n\npython review_coverage.py inventory REPO\npython review_coverage.py ledger FINDINGS.json CHECK --rows rows.json --attempts attempts.json\n\nInventory matches are candidates, not findings or proof of completeness. Review\naliases, wrappers, computed calls and unsupported languages manually as well.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {inventory,policy,ledger}\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n{\n  \"id\": \"B05.2\",\n  \"check\": \"The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.\",\n  \"coverage_required\": true,\n  \"verify\": \"1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For each value that lets an action skip approval (an internal recipient, a small amount, a safe path), read the comparison itself: answer no if a domain, host, or path is matched by suffix, prefix, or substring without an anchor, so `endswith('example.com')` also passes `evil-example.com`.\",\n  \"candidate_sites\": []\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"B01.1\",\n  \"check\": \"Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.\",\n  \"coverage_required\": true,\n  \"verify\": \"1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list.\",\n  \"candidate_sites\": []\n}\n{\n  \"brace_checklist\": \"2026-10-01\",\n  \"repo\": \"\",\n  \"commit\": null,\n  \"reviewed_at\": \"2026-10-20T12:58:54Z\",\n  \"scope\": \"TODO: what was reviewed and what wasn't.\",\n  \"review_protocol\": \"coverage-first-v1\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"item\": \"B01\",\n      \"gate\": \"G1\",\n      \"title\": \"Minimal tool surface\",\n      \"requirement\": \"List every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \\\"read-only.\\\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.\",\n      \"not_covered\": [\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly\",\n          \"owner\": \"E06\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"A token or credential with too much access\",\n          \"owner\": \"B02\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema\",\n          \"owner\": \"E04\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved\",\n          \"owner\": \"E03\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"problem\": \"Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers\",\n          \"owner\": \"E05\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"rating\": \"needs_evidence\",\n      \"confidence\": \"low